{"step_index":121,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T11:25:02Z","thinking":"Beginning to construct the decommissioning procedure, focusing on creating a well-structured document. The procedure needs a standard header; determining the best format for it. The language must be French, ensuring professional terminology. The image should be integrated logically within the document's flow.\n\n\nAnalyzing the provided metadata to define the scope and purpose of the decommissioning procedure, noting it covers Linux/Unix servers, various infrastructure components, and aims for data integrity. The RACI matrix highlights key roles and responsibilities across different teams. The workflow diagram underscores the sequential steps of the decommissioning process, with an emphasis on zero data loss and complete traceability.\n\n\nFocusing on the detailed workflow, beginning with the request stage (step 1), ensuring all required information is gathered in the ticket for decommissioning. Next, analyzing the impact and risks (step 2), a detailed technical audit of the target machine is required, including network connections, mounted shares, and reverse proxy configurations. This involves checking dependencies and scheduled tasks.\n\n\nConsidering detailed risk assessment and rollback strategy during the \"Analyze Impact\" phase (Step 2) of the workflow, covering critical aspects like authentication methods, database instances, and IaC inventories. Risk levels (1-4) must be assigned, along with impact estimations to inform the rollback plan.\n\nDetermining the appropriate quarantine period (15-30 days) after decommissioning, then creating the necessary CAB documentation. Document preparation will cover the change request to be presented before the CAB, outlining business, security, and compliance impacts.\n\nNext focus will be on formalizing the CAB validation process (Step 3), ensuring a clear decision-making pathway: Approval, Rejection, or Request for further information. A unique CAB approval number will be assigned if the request is approved.\n\nInitiating the \"Decommissioning Start\" phase (Step 4), confirming the transition to technical execution, sending notification to relevant teams, and locking the application access to prevent data modification before backup, are essential actions.\n\n\nUpdating the NetBox DCIM/IPAM system to reflect the decommissioning status and adding relevant metadata, including the CAB ticket number and requester. This ensures proper tracking of the decommissioned equipment and facilitates future reference.\n\nDeactivating monitoring systems by placing the target in maintenance mode to prevent unnecessary alerts and escalation during the shutdown process, covering alerts and system metrics. This includes tools within the enterprise supervision platforms.\n\nPerforming a final, comprehensive backup for data integrity and recovery purposes, including VM snapshots or copies, and ensuring the database backups are logical and consistent. This guarantees that data is preserved before the system is retired.\n\n\nFocusing on the final backup, which entails creating an encrypted archive of critical system directories and application data to a central storage location, also calculating and documenting the SHA256 hash for integrity verification. Determining the backup's retention period will be based on CAB-approved guidelines. Formal validation of the backup's success is mandatory.\n\nThe decommissioning's shutdown phase requires a systematic stop of all services and containers, followed by a clean OS shutdown. This involves disconnecting the host from infrastructure management, by removing it from the configuration and revoking automated access keys. Then, the system goes into a cold-shutdown quarantine for 15-30 days, preventing any disk operations.\n\n\nDisabling firewall rules is essential, identifying all rules related to the server's IP address and disabling them with a specific comment, and maintaining them disabled during the quarantine period. Permanent equipment removal depends on the type of server, whether it's a VMware VM or a physical server; the process includes deletion from disk or physical dismantling and certified data erasure, respectively.\n\n\nAnalyzing resource deallocation to include SAN LUN detachment/deletion, and NFS share removal from NAS devices. Network cleanup involves deleting DNS records (A, CNAME, PTR) and cleaning DHCP reservations.\n\nFinalizing the process involves firewall rule deletion, removing associated IP objects, and synchronizing security policy. The final step updates the decommissioned status in the inventory system, releases the IPAM, and completes a decommissioning report, detailing resource release and backup location.\n\n\nConcluding the decommissioning process with CAB ticket closure, and final notifications to all stakeholders to ensure transparency and accountability. A detailed emergency rollback procedure needs to be defined to address potential critical incidents during the quarantine period.\n\nThe rollback plan involves specific steps: signaling to Network Operations Center (NOC) and Linux Infra, immediate reactivation of firewall rules by Security Operations, server restart by Linux Infra, re-enabling monitoring by NOC Business Operations, and dependence analysis. A comprehensive operational checklist is needed to streamline and validate each decommissioning step.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/upload_procedure.py\nimport requests, os, json, sys\n\nTOKEN = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpZCI6IjY2ZDAwY2I0LWEzY2YtNDIyYS1hMjQ5LWVkMzA2NzEwZDdjZiIsImV4cGlyZXNBdCI6IjIwMjYtMTItMDhUMTA6NDU6MjcuNzkzWiIsInR5cGUiOiJzZXNzaW9uIiwic2VydmljZSI6ImF6dXJlIiwiaWF0IjoxNzg4ODY0MzI3fQ.tQTTmIeOP_H2i-10PROwjV-c_snixZFU5pG6CpKXXCk'\nBASE_URL = 'https://wiki.corp.intranet.local'\nIMAGE_PATH = '/home/koby/Downloads/Decom.excalidraw(1).png'\n\nheaders = {\n    'Authorization': f'Bearer {TOKEN}',\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json'\n}\n\n# 1. Upload attachment\nfilesize = os.path.getsize(IMAGE_PATH)\nfilename = 'Decom_workflow.png'\n\nres = requests.post(\n    f'{BASE_URL}/api/attachments.create',\n    headers=headers,\n    json={'name': filename, 'size': filesize, 'contentType': 'image/png'},\n    verify=False\n)\nif res.status_code != 200 or not res.json().get('ok'):\n    print('Failed attachments.create:', res.text)\n    sys.exit(1)\n\natt_data = res.json()['data']\nattachment_id = att_data['attachment']['id']\nattachment_url = att_data['attachment']['url']\nupload_url = att_data['uploadUrl']\nif not upload_url.startswith('http'):\n    upload_url = BASE_URL + upload_url\n\nform_data = att_data['form']\nwith open(IMAGE_PATH, 'rb') as f:\n    files = {'file': (filename, f, 'image/png')}\n    up_res = requests.post(\n        upload_url,\n        data=form_data,\n        files=files,\n        headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {TOKEN}'},\n        verify=False\n    )\nif up_res.status_code != 200:\n    print('Failed file upload:', up_res.status_code, up_res.text)\n    sys.exit(1)\n\nprint(f\"Attachment uploaded successfully! ID={attachment_id}, URL={attachment_url}\")\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/upload_procedure.py\n","Cwd":"/home/koby","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Uploading image attachment","toolSummary":"Upload image attachment"}}]}
